=base:main-content.cmark

# Guides

These pages are generated straight from the `resources/` directory of the
[salmon repository](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/salmon/tree/master/resources)
— the repository is always the canonical source, and may be ahead of what is
published here.

- [**The salmon model**](/docs-salmon-core.html) — the general,
  domain-independent model that lives in `salmon-core` (`Graph`, `OpGraph`,
  `Track`, `Eval`), why it is shaped this way, and where else it could be
  pointed. Start here for the *why*.
- [**How to write and test salmon ops**](/docs-howto-ops.html) — the
  cookbook: copy the patterns, adapt the names, and you produce a valid `Op`.
  Written to be usable as a reference even by less-context-heavy tooling.
  Start here for the *how*.
- [**Salmon ops patterns**](/docs-salmon-ops-patterns.html) — recurring
  shapes worth reusing across recipes, such as a one-time privileged
  bootstrap.
- [**`run serve`: convergence and supervision**](/docs-serve-supervision.html)
  — what the long-running mode gives you for free (convergence,
  `status`/`force`/`pause`, self-healing on re-declaration), how to try it,
  the line protocol, the HTTP API and event stream, the web UI, TLS over
  TCP, pull mode and the status sink, and what to decorate a node with
  (`check`, `Supervision`, `managed`) to get more out of it.
- [**A Postgres pair whose primary is declared, not discovered**](/docs-postgres-pair.html)
  — what a switchover does step by step, when the recipe refuses and why,
  and how to watch a client keep writing across one with the
  `salmon-toy-qemu-pg-ha` demo.
- [**Validating the GCP builtins against a real project**](/docs-gcp-toy-validation.html)
  — the playbook for exercising the `Gcp.*` builtins in a sandbox you are
  willing to destroy, with the `salmon-gcp-toy` binary.

Two more documents exist only in the repository:

- [`CLAUDE.md`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/salmon/blob/master/CLAUDE.md)
  — the architecture module by module and the repository-level conventions
  (package layering, idempotency, failure propagation), for anyone, human or
  AI, working in the codebase.
- [`CHANGELOG.md`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/salmon/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md)
  — release history.

